  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA decaying MCV will move south out of KS early Wednesday \nmorning just after midnight. There is the possibility of a few \nshowers moving into SE KS/SW MO as the MCV dives south into \ncentral OK just before sunrise. Leftover boundaries from the\ndying MCV will push east through the day into an unstable (4000\nJ/kg MUCAPE) and moist atmosphere (dewpoints in the 70s), with\n0-6km bulk shear of 25-35 kts. These leftover boundaries will \ncollide with an incoming cold front that will round the base of \na mid- level trough that's currently situated over the Great \nLakes region. This will initiate showers and thunderstorms \n(30-60%) across the area in the afternoon and evening hours \nmainly from 2-8 PM. The area with the highest threat for severe \nweather will be south of Highway 60 near the MO/AR border as AR \nhas the better environment with higher dewpoints and a \nstationary front hanging out over the central part of the state.\nMain threat is 60 mph damaging wind gusts, secondary threat is \n1 inch hail, and there is a very low threat for a tornado or \ntwo. PWATs will be near 2 inches so any areas that see rain may \nsee nuisance flooding as well.\n\nThere are still some uncertainties with Wednesday's forecast.\nBoundaries that move in off the MCV will dictate where storms\ninitiate, high clouds may impact our instability, and if morning\nshowers linger longer than anticipated this will keep us more\nstable. The timing of the cold front will also be a factor\ntomorrow. \n\nThe rest of the week into the weekend is forecast to remain dry. \nLooking ahead to the start of September, CPC outlook for the \nfirst week of September doesn't bode well for the folks who are \neager for fall to arrive. The 6-10 day temperature outlook has a\n65% chance for above normal temperatures (avg = 82-87 degrees) \nand equal chances to see above/below normal rainfall accumulation\n(avg = 0.73\").",
